    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1186/s12982-026-01477-1",
    "abstract": "This study focuses on the composite health vulnerability assessment of the north-eastern community of India, with maximum possible socio-economic, demographic, epidemiological, and climatic parameters. We have used the vulnerability indices as developed by Diouf et al. (in Handbook of climate change adaptation. Springer: Berlin; https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-38670-1_42 , 2015), Hahn et al. (in Glob Environ Change; 19(1): 74\u201388, 2009), and Weis et al. (in Clim Change; 136(3\u20134): 615\u2013629, 2016) and IPCC 2007 Third Report, with some modifications in methodology, components (13), and sub-components (90) to thoroughly investigate the health vulnerability. Socio-economic and demographic data are used to find the sensitivity and adaptive capacity, whereas climatic and disease-related data are used to find the exposure index. Techniques like standard deviation, coefficient of variation, and absolute change in climatic parameters, along with normalisation of data for indexing, are used. The key finding of the research is that Arunachal Pradesh has the highest adaptive capacity but also high sensitivity, whereas Assam has the lowest adaptive capacity and high sensitivity. Overall, Arunachal Pradesh has the lowest vulnerability because of its highest adaptive capacity, although it has high sensitivity, while Assam has the highest vulnerability because of its low adaptive capacity. Manipur is in the upper moderate range, and the rest of the states are in the moderate range. In this vulnerability assessment, it is concluded that, along with climatic variability, assessment of social and economic parameters is also crucial to know the actual health vulnerability of the area.",
    "field": "Health vulnerability assessment",
    "problem": "Existing health vulnerability assessments for North East India are scarce and utilize a limited number of indicators, resulting in an incomplete and less reliable picture of the actual multidimensional health vulnerability of the area.",
    "method": "This study assesses health vulnerability in North East India by adapting four vulnerability frameworks (IPCC, Hahn, Diouf, Weis) with 90 socio-economic, demographic, and climatic sub-components to create composite indices for exposure, sensitivity, and adaptive capacity.",
    "task": "The task is to conduct a composite health vulnerability assessment of the north-eastern community of India by integrating socio-economic, demographic, epidemiological, and climatic parameters into established vulnerability frameworks.",
